[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Advanced TypeScript: Keyof, Indexed Access, and Mapped Types"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"TypeScript offers powerful type operators that allow you to construct flexible and robust type definitions based on existing types. In this tutorial, we will explore keyof, indexed access types, and mapped types to elevate your type safety."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"1. The Keyof Operator"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The keyof operator takes an object type and produces a string or numeric literal union of its keys. This is extremely useful for ensuring that functions only accept valid property names of a given object."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"interface User {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

type UserKeys = keyof User; // Result: 'id' | 'name' | 'email'"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Using Keyof in Functions"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can combine keyof with generics to create type-safe property getters:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}"},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"2. Indexed Access Types"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Indexed access types allow you to look up a specific property type on another type, using syntax similar to JavaScript property access."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"interface Person {
  age: number;
  name: string;
  address: {
    street: string;
    city: string;
  };
}

type AgeType = Person['age']; // number
type CityType = Person['address']['city']; // string"},{"order":12,"type":"heading","text":"3. Mapped Types"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Mapped types let you create new types based on old ones by iterating over property keys. They use a generic type containing a union of property keys, typically produced via keyof."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"type Readonly<T> = {
  readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K];
};

type Optional<T> = {
  [K in keyof T]?: T[K];
};"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Modifier Remapping with As"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also remap keys in mapped types using the 'as' clause, which is helpful for transforming property names dynamically."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"type GetterProps<T> = {
  [K in keyof T as `get${Capitalize<string & K>}`]: () => T[K];
};

interface Product {
  id: number;
  title: string;
}

type ProductGetters = GetterProps<Product>;
// Results in: { getId: () => number; getTitle: () => string; }"},{"order":18,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining keyof, indexed access, and mapped types, you can eliminate code duplication and build self-updating types that automatically reflect changes in your data models."}]
